1
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:14,990
Two dead objects. Collision course.

2
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:16,280
Whose are they?

3
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:18,940
Nobody's. Dead thirty years.

4
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,820
So whose job is it?

5
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:26,250
Temi, wake up. Two dead
objects, forty hours to impact.

6
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:28,070
What time is it?

7
00:00:27,990 --> 00:00:29,290
Time to launch.

8
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:32,150
Winds are at the limit. Your call.

9
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:33,760
We go. Light it.

10
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:48,510
Tug control, we have visual contact...

11
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:53,510
Copy that, manoeuvring
thrusters engaged...

12
00:00:53,890 --> 00:00:56,120
No docking port. Nothing to grab.

13
00:00:56,290 --> 00:00:57,350
Then we abort.

14
00:00:57,490 --> 00:00:59,940
No. We grab it anyway.

15
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:03,450
We have it.

16
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:09,220
We cannot bring it down.
Nine tonnes. Not even close.

17
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:12,020
So we caught it for nothing?

18
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:15,600
You don't have to bring it
down. You have to make it late.

19
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:16,800
Late? How late?

20
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:21,770
One centimetre a second, twenty hours
early, is two kilometres at the crossing.

21
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,380
There we go, come on now.

22
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:31,420
Closest approach in three... two...

23
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:35,600
Two point one kilometres.

24
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:41,060
Every space agency on
Earth is calling you.

25
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:42,510
Then it's somebody's job now.

26
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:48,600
Look, Dad. A shooting star.
